t": "This is captured in the formula of one Advaitin: “[He] is never born again who knows that he is the only one in all beings like the ether and that all beings are in him” (Upadesa Sahasri XVII.69). Many thinkers in the history of Indian philosophy have held that there is an important connection between action and liberation. In contrast, Sankara rejects the …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "manual", "page_or_section": "IEP: Error, True Knowledge and Practical Teachings", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Adi Shankaracharya: Shankara rejects the view that liberation comes from combining Vedic action with knowledge; knowledge of Brahman alone liberates, while disciplined action prepares the mind.", "source_id": "a2a0752d-ffce-4a6f-b5a9-77186913ac3e", "textual_layer": "Academic philosophy reference article"}], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "Sangeetha Menon", "authority_metadata": {"verified_at": "2026-10-13"}, "citation": "Sangeetha Menon. Advaita Vedanta. Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy / National Institute of Advanced Studies. https://iep.utm.edu/advaita-vedanta/", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": null, "publisher_or_institution": "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy / National Institute of Advanced Studies", "rights_or_access_note": "Publicly accessible source; short research excerpts only.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "institutional", "storage_path": null, "title": "Advaita Vedanta", "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/advaita-vedanta/"}, "source_id": "a2a0752d-ffce-4a6f-b5a9-77186913ac3e"}, {"evidence_excerpt": "700 C.E.), whose interpretations of the Upanisads made a major impact on the Indian philosophical tradition in the centuries after his lifetime and continued to dominate readings of the texts throughout the 19th and early 20th centuries. Sankara was the main proponent of Advaita Vedanta, which put forth a position of non-dualism. According to Sankara the …", "l