 of, the Divine Mother. In 1976, Neevel built upon Zimmer''s interpretation to argue that Ramakrishna''s view of the ultimate was best understood as a form of Tantric nondualism, not a Vedāntic one. Among other things, this …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": null, "page_or_section": "Encyclopedia of Religion", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Ramakrishna Paramahamsa: Walter Neevel (1976), building on Heinrich Zimmer, argued that Ramakrishna''s view of the ultimate is best understood as Tantric nondualism rather than Vedantic, which fits his concern with worldly activity as the Mother''s playful power.", "source_id": "d5a13923-a194-487e-8a9b-0d61f574d7e7", "textual_layer": "Scholarly encyclopedia entry by university historians of religion"}], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "Walter G. Neevel Jr. (1987); revised by Brian A. Hatcher (2005)", "authority_metadata": {"verified_at": "2026-10-12"}, "citation": "Walter G. Neevel Jr. (1987); revised by Brian A. Hatcher (2005). Ramakrishna (Encyclopedia of Religion, 2nd edition). Macmillan Reference / Gale; read through Encyclopedia.com. https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/philosophy-and-religion/hinduism-biographies/ramakrishna", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": 2005, "publisher_or_institution": "Macmillan Reference / Gale; read through Encyclopedia.com", "rights_or_access_note": "Reference entry; short research excerpts only.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "institutional", "storage_path": null, "title": "Ramakrishna (Encyclopedia of Religion, 2nd edition)", "url": "https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/philosophy-and-religion/hinduism-biographies/ramakrishna"}, "source_id": "d5a13923-a194-487e-8a9b-0d61f574d7e7"}], "unmatched_passage_links": []}'::jsonb,'draft','ai_candidate');
insert into original_claims values ('38295f1a-da7d-42e1-9909-ae5530af2fb3','{"approval_origin": "ai_candidate", "certainty": "medium", "claim_id": "38295f1a-da7d-42e1-9909-ae5530af2fb3", "claim_kind": "uncertain", "claim_relations": [], "claim_text": "The reception of Kali''s Child split: in India attempts were made to ban it and Kripal was threatened, while most US scholars of religion praised it; a Vedanta Society reviewer likewise reports calls for the Indian government to ban it.", "current_status": "draft", "epistemics": {"contract_basis": null, "contract_satisfied": false, "contract_status": "NEEDS_TYPE_REVIEW", "epistemic_type": null, "proposed_type": "CONTESTED", "reviewer_kind": null, "type_origin": "deterministic", "type_review_status": "needs_type_review"}, "evidence_flags": ["Missing assigned epistemic classification"], "evidence_type": "contested", "lens": {"field_id": "cd01e895-3db1-4ce4-bdfa-bf792abf0284", "field_label": "Challenges and contested interpretations", "parameter_id": "8f0225f6-5acd-4481-8c81-428a067efede", "parameter_name": "Challenges and contested interpretations"}, "parent_claim_id": null, "personality_id": "9ef04ec3-7bf1-4210-a951-1d6da7b2fc99", "personality_name": "Ramakrishna Paramahamsa", "reviewer_decision": null, "sources": [{"evidence_excerpt": "Much of the wrath was directed at The Statesman for publishing the review (“filth,” “trash,” “garbage,” “rubbish”), although Kripal of course received a fair share of vilification (“sick,” “diseased,” “perverted”). There were calls for the government of India to ban the book. Kripal himself received a great deal of hate mail (“Dear Mr. Perverter”), and even …", "link_id": "f72edf8f-d01d-4735-af75-43801ea59903", "page_or_section": "There were calls for the government of India to ban the book.", "passages": [], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "William Page", "authority_metadata": {"verified_at": "2026-10-12"}, "citation": "William Page. Review: Interpreting Ramakrishna: Kali''s Child Revisited. American Vedantist. https://americanvedantist.org/2010/media-reviews/reviewinterpreting-ramakrishna-kali%E2%80%99s-child-revisited/", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": 2010, "publisher_or_institution": "American Vedantist", "rights_or_access_note": "Insider review; used for the history of the controversy and flagged as such.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "institutional", "storage_path": null, "title": "Review: Interpreting Ramakrishna: Kali''s Child Revisited", "url": "https://americanvedantist.org/2010/media-reviews/reviewinterpreting-ramakrishna-kali%E2%80%99s-child-revisited/"}, "source_id": "547c1337-624e-4cad-9ef9-5080e5d3308e"}, {"evidence_excerpt": "Some critics treated Kripal to the same facile psychoanalysis that they accused him of applying to Ramakrishna. Attempts to ban his book went all the way to the Indian Parliament. He was even personally threatened in the closest thing to a fatwah that one can imagine in the land of Gandhi.Kripal''s critics within the academy were less emotional, disagreeing …", "link_id": "155b6635-1a86-45a1-aed3-b78cd11a287c", "page_or_section": "Los Angeles Review of Books, 2011", "passages": [{"chunk_id": "8bfac353-ec78-495d-b2af-e19b5012fb31", "confidence": null, "excerpt": "Some critics treated Kripal to the same facile psychoanalysis that they accused him of applying to Ramakrishna. Attempts to ban his book went all the way to the Indian Parliament. He was even personally threatened in the closest thing to a fatwah that one can imagine in the land of Gandhi.Kripal''s critics within the academy were less emotional, disagreeing …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": null, "page_or_section": "Los Angeles Review of Books, 2011", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Ramakrishna Paramahamsa: The reception of Kali''s Child split: in India attempts were made to ban it and Kripal was threatened, while most US scholars of religion praised it; a V