e points of view.\"However, Ramakrishna''s gospel of the truth within all religions is not based simply on the belief that they all lead to the realization of the same formless absolute, in which all difference is transcended and negated. Rather, it is based upon his own …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": null, "page_or_section": "Encyclopedia of Religion", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Ramakrishna Paramahamsa: Scholars read his gospel of the truth of all religions as based less on the belief that all lead to one formless Absolute than on his own experience of divine power at work in every manifest form, in line with his saying yato mat, tato path (\"as many paths as points of view\").", "source_id": "d5a13923-a194-487e-8a9b-0d61f574d7e7", "textual_layer": "Scholarly encyclopedia entry by university historians of religion"}], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "Walter G. Neevel Jr. (1987); revised by Brian A. Hatcher (2005)", "authority_metadata": {"verified_at": "2026-10-12"}, "citation": "Walter G. Neevel Jr. (1987); revised by Brian A. Hatcher (2005). Ramakrishna (Encyclopedia of Religion, 2nd edition). Macmillan Reference / Gale; read through Encyclopedia.com. https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/philosophy-and-religion/hinduism-biographies/ramakrishna", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": 2005, "publisher_or_institution": "Macmillan Reference / Gale; read through Encyclopedia.com", "rights_or_access_note": "Reference entry; short research excerpts only.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "institutional", "storage_path": null, "title": "Ramakrishna (Encyclopedia of Religion, 2nd edition)", "url": "https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/philosophy-and-religion/hinduism-biographies/ramakrishna"}, "source_id": "d5a13923-a194-487e-8a9b-0d61f574d7e7"}], "unmatched_passage_links": []}'::jsonb,'draft','ai_candidate');
