"Rigveda — Mandala 3, Sukta 33"}], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "Traditional Vedic text", "authority_metadata": {}, "citation": "Rigveda 3.33, Shakala Samhita. Vedic Heritage Portal.", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": null, "publisher_or_institution": "Vedic Heritage Portal, IGNCA, Ministry of Culture, Government of India", "rights_or_access_note": null, "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "primary_text", "storage_path": null, "title": "Rigveda Shakala Samhita — Mandala 3, Sukta 33", "url": "https://vedicheritage.gov.in/samhitas/rigveda/shakala-samhita/rigveda-shakala-samhita-mandal-03-sukta-033/"}, "source_id": "c23e92e1-d8a8-4e87-8476-09120bed5d01"}], "unmatched_passage_links": [{"chunk_id": "5b132fbf-86c5-4d46-a458-d5d9f306311d", "excerpt": "अच्छा सिन्धुं मातृतमामयासं विपाशमुर्वीं सुभगामगन्म । वत्समिव मातरा संरिहाणे समानं योनिमनु संचरन्ती acchā sindhuṃ mātṛtamāmayāsaṃ vipāśamurvīṃ subhagāmaganma | vatsamiva mātarā saṃrihāṇe samānaṃ yonimanu saṃcarantī I have attained the most maternal River, we have approached Vipas, the broad, the blessed. Licking as ''twere their calf the pair of Mothers flow …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "3.33.3", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": null, "publication_year": null, "publisher_or_institution": null, "rights_or_access_note": null, "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "primary_text", "title": "Maṇḍala 3 — 62 hymns of the Viśvāmitra family (incl. Gāyatrī) — Rigveda — Swaveda", "url": "https://www.swaveda.com/texts/rigveda/3/"}, "source_id": "681b7993-1899-497d-864c-287d302819ec"}, {"chunk_id": "9f7f8810-2339-486b-a933-2e3771e02f8a", "excerpt": "प्र पर्वतानामुशती उपस्थादश्वे इव विषिते हासमाने । गावेव शुभ्रे मातरा रिहाणे विपाट् छुतुद्री पयसा जवेते pra parvatānāmuśatī upasthādaśve iva viṣite hāsamāne | gāveva śubhre mātarā rihāṇe vipāṭ chutudrī payasā javete FORTH from the bosom of the mountains, eager as two swift mares with loosened rein contending, Like two bright mother cows who lick their …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "3.33.1", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": null, "publication_year": null, "publisher_or_institution": null, "rights_or_access_note": null, "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "primary_text", "title": "Maṇḍala 3 — 62 hymns of the Viśvāmitra family (incl. Gāyatrī) — Rigveda — Swaveda", "url": "https://www.swaveda.com/texts/rigveda/3/"}, "source_id": "681b7993-1899-497d-864c-287d302819ec"}]}'::jsonb,'approved','system_test_bulk');
insert into original_claims values ('d5d917d2-47a1-4747-ae77-5407b5a23e34','{"approval_origin": "system_test_bulk", "certainty": "high", "claim_id": "d5d917d2-47a1-4747-ae77-5407b5a23e34", "claim_kind": "traditional_account", "claim_relations": [], "claim_text": "Vishvamitra acknowledges King Dasharatha as belonging to a great dynasty and having been tutored by Sage Vashishta.", "current_status": "approved", "epistemics": {"contract_basis": "tradition", "contract_satisfied": true, "contract_status": "SUPPORTED", "epistemic_type": "TRADITIONAL_NARRATIVE", "proposed_type": "TRADITIONAL_NARRATIVE", "reviewer_kind": null, "type_origin": "deterministic", "type_review_status": "auto_assigned"}, "evidence_flags": ["Current approval origin does not establish strict verification", "Retention/access rights note missing for an associated source", "Claim-passage link points to a source absent from claim-source links"], "evidence_type": "traditional_narrative", "lens": {"field_id": "a38a1691-19f9-491e-b8c7-efef4de2d5e4", "field_label": "Ministers, advisors, and gurus", "parameter_id": "866fcbd7-b099-4d74-a1f5-75ba01941edf", "parameter_name": "Queens, Royal Family, Ministers, Advisors, and Gurus"}, "parent_claim_id": null, "personality_id": "2853f4ed-98b1-4ef8-9f30-30f85d42edad", "personality_name": "Vishvamitra", "reviewer_decision": null, "sources": [{"evidence_excerpt": "Sage Vishvamitra asks King Dasharatha to send Rama to protect the Vedic ritual   that is being conducted by him, from the demons that are constantly disrupting   it. The sage asserts King Dasharatha that he and the other sages in Dasharatha''s   court know the capabilities of Rama and though Rama is in his adolescence, he   can easily kill the demons. But …", "link_id": "b2b6158c-c905-448b-a952-566db1efad08", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "passages": [{"chunk_id": "49491f41-5500-43be-977a-a8f12fc6841f", "confidence": 0.99, "excerpt": "\"At the near end of almost completed ritual of mine two valiant and well-trained   demons, namely Mareecha and Subaahu, are drenching the Altar of Fire with streams   of meat and blood. [1-19-5, 6a]  अवधूते तथाभूते तस्मिन्नियमनिश्चये || १-१९-६  कृतश्रमो निरुत्साहस्तस्माद्देशादपाक्रमे |  6b, 7a. tasmin niyamanishchaye = that, ritual vow; avadhuute   = …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "section_metadata", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Introduction", "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda, Sarga 19"}, {"chunk_id": "9374da7d-e000-40d3-83fc-7d752aa7f76c", "confidence": 0.99, "excerpt": "Sage Vishvamitra asks King Dasharatha to send Rama to protect the Vedic ritual   that is being conducted by him, from the demons that are constantly disrupting   it. The sage asserts King Dasharatha that he and the other sages in Dasharatha''s   court know the capabilities of Rama and though Rama is in his adolescence, he   can easily kill the demons. But …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "section_metadata", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Introduction", "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda, Sarga 19"}, {"chunk_id": "b1bc66f5-1954-483e-a7db-b00c168c90df", "confidence": 0.914838709677419, "excerpt": "Now Rama''s reputation is going to be enhanced by Sage Vishvamitra in eliminating   demoness Tataka, in releasing Ahalya from her curse, breaking Shiva''s bow, marriage   with Seetha, rebuffing Parashuraama etc. And also Vishvamitra leaves the decision   to Dasharatha, ''if only your ministers, clergy as well political, permit then   alone you send Rama with …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "curated_source_token_overlap", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Introduction", "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda, Sarga 19"}], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "Valmiki", "authority_metadata": {}, "citation": "Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Sarga 19 (https://valmikiramayan.net/utf8/baala/sarga19/balasans19.htm)", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": null, "publisher_or_institution": "Valmiki Ramayana project", "rights_or_access_note": null, "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_article", "storage_path": null, "title": "Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Sarga 19", "url": "https://valmikiramayan.net/utf8/baala/sarga19/balasans19.htm"}, "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478"}], "unmatched_passage_links": [{"chunk_id": "1c62b977-f2a3-404b-bb2e-32c40683507d", "excerpt": "50 Viśvāmitra, this essay will recover two distinct interpretive voices in the Sanskrit Mahābhārata: one that spotlights the extraordinary ascetic power behind his social rupture, and another, louder one that minimizes the social impact of this counter- normative figure by insisting that his varṇa change never actually happened. Bhīṣma’s reply to …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "p. 50 (PDF page 10)", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "Adheesh Sathaye", "publication_year": 2007, "publisher_or_institution": "Acta Orientalia Vilnensia / Vilnius University Press", "rights_or_access_note": "Official journal diamond open access, CC BY 4.0: https://www.journals.vu.lt/acta-orientalia-vilnensia/journalpolicy", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_article", "title": "How to become a Brahman: The construction of varṇa as social place in the Mahābhārata’s legends of Viśvāmitra", "url": "https://www.journals.vu.lt/acta-orientalia-vilnensia/article/download/3751/5207"}, "source_id": "06fec486-e5af-4c7e-95db-afbb2e237c75"}, {"chunk_id": "2990dad2-03cc-4d13-8afc-f8705e5b1244", "excerpt": "RISE OF KSATRIYAN .BRAHMANS 245 is no suggestion that there was any difficulty in the assumption of brahmanhood in such cases, and hymns composed by such persons were admitted into the Rigveda. Princes who became rishis in the earliest times are often described as having qualified themselves by long austerities, but, apart from the general statement about …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "p. 245 (scan leaf 257)", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "F. E. Pargiter", "publication_year": 1922, "publisher_or_institution": "Oxford University Press (London: Humphrey Milford)", "rights_or_access_note": "Rights-clear public-domain scan (archive.org item metadata: NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT; scanned by University of Toronto Robarts Library). Only pages naming Vishvamitra and relevant to approved claims were ingested.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_book", "title": "Ancient Indian Historical Tradition", "url": "https://archive.org/details/ancientindianhis00parguoft"}, "source_id": "70bed1d6-b121-41fb-9ec4-650a746f154c"}, {"chunk_id": "3a7187c0-39ae-483b-8748-f00255c10a80", "excerpt": "the story of Vasistha, Visvamitra and king Trisanku in the Maha- bharata and Ramriyana,^ that of Visvamitra and king- Hariscandra in the Mfirkandeya (7 and 8), and that of king Hariscandra, Rohita and Sunahsepa in the Aitareya Brahmana- and Sahkhjiyana Srauta Sutra.\" Others are plainly ksatriya tales, such as the ballad of king Satyaviata-Trisiihku, …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "scan leaf 71", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "F. E. Pargiter", "publication_year": 1922, "publisher_or_institution": "Oxford University Press (London: Humphrey Milford)", "rights_or_access_note": "Rights-clear public-domain scan (archive.org item metadata: NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT; scanned by University of Toronto Robarts Library). Only pages naming Vishvamitra and relevant to approved claims were ingested.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_book", "title": "Ancient Indian Historical Tradition", "url": "https://archive.org/details/ancientindianhis00parguoft"}, "source_id": "70bed1d6-b121-41fb-9ec4-650a746f154c"}, {"chunk_id": "5b2883d3-63d7-4bfd-a637-3823fd3f91cb", "excerpt": "deprived o£ the kingdom and the priesthood, bore deadly enmity against Visvamitra, and sought revenge by denying his brahman- hood. Vasistha^s personal name was Devaraj. The story goes on thus.^ On Satyavrata''s death his son Hari- seandra was placed on the throne by Visvamitra, and Visvamitra offered the rajasuya sacrifice for him. But Vasistha''s hatred and …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "p. 206 (scan leaf 218)", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "F. E. Pargiter", "publication_year": 1922, "publisher_or_institution": "Oxford University Press (London: Humphrey Milford)", "rights_or_access_note": "Rights-clear public-domain scan (archive.org item metadata: NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT; scanned by University of Toronto Robarts Library). Only pages naming Vishvamitra and relevant to approved claims were ingested.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_book", "title": "Ancient Indian Historical Tradition", "url": "https://archive.org/details/ancientindianhis00parguoft"}, "source_id": "70bed1d6-b121-41fb-9ec4-650a746f154c"}, {"chunk_id": "7e87769d-290f-443e-81c3-bfc5c2bcc23d", "excerpt": "honour o£ ksatrijas. It notices rislns who came into contact with kinsrs. but otherwise is not much concerned with the life and thoughts of rishis. Ksatriya tales do often indulge in the mar- vellous, but their marvels are generally mere exaggeration without any didactic purpose; and ksatriya tradition, even when magnifying the glory of kings, does not …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "scan leaf 72", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "F. E. Pargiter", "publication_year": 1922, "publisher_or_institution": "Oxford University Press (London: Humphrey Milford)", "rights_or_access_note": "Rights-clear public-domain scan (archive.org item metadata: NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT; scanned by University of Toronto Robarts Library). Only pages naming Vishvamitra and relevant to approved claims were ingested.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_book", "title": "Ancient Indian Historical Tradition", "url": "https://archive.org/details/ancientindianhis00parguoft"}, "source_id": "70bed1d6-b121-41fb-9ec4-650a746f154c"}, {"chunk_id": "abd12ab9-afef-40d4-8929-ffdcd5e9828e", "excerpt": "The next group of synchronisms is that in which Visvamitra and his nephew Jamadagni are the central figures. King Krtavlrya of the Haihayas had the Bhargavas as his priests and enriched them. His successors tried to recover the wealth, but the Bhargavas resisted. The Haihayas maltreated them, and the Bhargavas fled to other countries for safety.^ Gadhi or …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "p. 151 (scan leaf 163)", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "F. E. Pargiter", "publication_year": 1922, "publisher_or_institution": "Oxford University Press (London: Humphrey Milford)", "rights_or_access_note": "Rights-clear public-domain scan (archive.org item metadata: NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT; scanned by University of Toronto Robarts Library). Only pages naming Vishvamitra and relevant to approved claims were ingested.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_book", "title": "Ancient Indian Historical Tradition", "url": "https://archive.org/details/ancientindianhis00parguoft"}, "source_id": "70bed1d6-b121-41fb-9ec4-650a746f154c"}, {"chunk_id": "b303cbde-ba21-4801-a8ca-bd64aaeecf24", "excerpt": "52 society.43 the legend induces a social rupture—the inescapable radicality of a human body out of place. Though we are not given extensive detail about the bodies of Vasiṣṭha and Viśvāmitra in either epic version of the story, a manifest opposition between their Brahman and Kṣatriya bodies is created through formulaic epithets and dialogues in the …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "p. 52 (PDF page 12)", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "Adheesh Sathaye", "publication_year": 2007, "publisher_or_institution": "Acta Orientalia Vilnensia / Vilnius University Press", "rights_or_access_note": "Official journal diamond open access, CC BY 4.0: https://www.journals.vu.lt/acta-orientalia-vilnensia/journalpolicy", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_article", "title": "How to become a Brahman: The construction of varṇa as social place in the Mahābhārata’s legends of Viśvāmitra", "url": "https://www.journals.vu.lt/acta-orientalia-vilnensia/article/download/3751/5207"}, "source_id": "06fec486-e5af-4c7e-95db-afbb2e237c75"}, {"chunk_id": "d626535b-2b36-41ca-8aad-62c698eaf849", "excerpt": "their right to perform sacrifices, such as king Janamejaya Ill''s dispute/ for it seems that in early times kings themselves sacrificed. These two kinds were the analogues of disputes and contests in Europe between the temporal and spiritual powers. The third is the only kind that concerns us here, and of this kind there is really none, as far as I am aware, …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "p. 244 (scan leaf 256)", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "F. E. Pargiter", "publication_year": 1922, "publisher_or_institution": "Oxford University Press (London: Humphrey Milford)", "rights_or_access_note": "Rights-clear public-domain scan (archive.org item metadata: NOT_IN_COPYRIGHT; scanned by University of Toronto Robarts Library). Only pages naming Vishvamitra and relevant to approved claims were ingested.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_book", "title": "Ancient Indian Historical Tradition", "url": "https://archive.org/details/ancientindianhis00parguoft"}, "source_id": "70bed1d6-b121-41fb-9ec4-650a746f154c"}, {"chunk_id": "e84c8f68-240a-4c1b-8129-a19cf5bf1503", "excerpt": "54 away, Vasiṣṭha informs her that as a ‘merciful Brahman [kṣamāvān brāhmaṇa]’, he is powerless to stop the king’s use of force (Mbh 1.165.24d). ‘the strength [bala] of Kṣatriyas is splendor [tejas]’, explains the sage, ‘while the strength of Brahmans is mercy [kṣamā]; mercy has possessed me, so you should go with him if you like’ (Mbh 1.165.28). The …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "page_or_section": "p. 54 (PDF page 14)", "relationship": "context", "source": {"author": "Adheesh Sathaye", "publication_year": 2007, "publisher_or_institution": "Acta Orientalia Vilnensia / Vilnius University Press", "rights_or_access_note": "Official journal diamond open access, CC BY 4.0: https://www.journals.vu.lt/acta-orientalia-vilnensia/journalpolicy", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_article", "title": "How to become a Brahman: The construction of varṇa as social place in the Mahābhārata’s legends of Viśvāmitra", "url": "https://www.journals.vu.lt/acta-orientalia-vilnensia/article/download/3751/5207"}, "source_id": "06fec486-e5af-4c7e-95db-afbb2e237c75"}]}'::jsonb,'approved','system_test_bulk');
insert into original_claims values ('0b71d9d8-29f4-40e5-a464-005f435fc2a4','{"approval_origin": "system_test_bulk", "certainty": "high", "claim_id": "0b71d9d8-29f4-40e5-a464-005f435fc2a4", "claim_kind": "traditional_account", "claim_relations": [], "claim_text": "Vishvamitra urges King Dasharatha to remain truthful to his promises and take a decision to resolve the obstruction to his ritual.", "current_status": "approved", "epistemics": {"contract_basis": "tradition", "contract_satisfied": true, "contract_status": "SUPPORTED", "epistemic_type": "TRADITIONAL_NARRATIVE", "proposed_type": "TRADITIONAL_NARRATIVE", "reviewer_kind": null, "type_origin": "deterministic", "type_review_status": "auto_assigned"}, "evidence_flags": ["Current approval origin does not establish strict verification", "Retention/access rights note missing for an associated source"], "evidence_type": "traditional_narrative", "lens": {"field_id": "de20bc35-2696-4a80-b095-614bb650a581", "field_label": "Advisory relationships with kings or dynasties", "parameter_id": "700ce11a-bd45-4116-99a4-a246dc97c505", "parameter_name": "Relational / Social Role"}, "parent_claim_id": null, "personality_id": "2853f4ed-98b1-4ef8-9f30-30f85d42edad", "personality_name": "Vishvamitra", "reviewer_decision": null, "sources": [{"evidence_excerpt": "Sage Vishvamitra asks King Dasharatha to send Rama to protect the Vedic ritual   that is being conducted by him, from the demons that are constantly disrupting   it. The sage asserts King Dasharatha that he and the other sages in Dasharatha''s   court know the capabilities of Rama and though Rama is in his adolescence, he   can easily kill the demons. But …", "link_id": "1c9dfb82-70dd-4e2a-a94c-6690b7fbbe60", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "passages": [{"chunk_id": "49491f41-5500-43be-977a-a8f12fc6841f", "confidence": 0.99, "excerpt": "\"At the near end of almost completed ritual of mine two valiant and well-trained   demons, namely Mareecha and Subaahu, are drenching the Altar of Fire with streams   of meat and blood. [1-19-5, 6a]  अवधूते तथाभूते तस्मिन्नियमनिश्चये || १-१९-६  कृतश्रमो निरुत्साहस्तस्माद्देशादपाक्रमे |  6b, 7a. tasmin niyamanishchaye = that, ritual vow; avadhuute   = …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "section_metadata", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Introduction", "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda, Sarga 19"}, {"chunk_id": "9374da7d-e000-40d3-83fc-7d752aa7f76c", "confidence": 0.99, "excerpt": "Sage Vishvamitra asks King Dasharatha to send Rama to protect the Vedic ritual   that is being conducted by him, from the demons that are constantly disrupting   it. The sage asserts King Dasharatha that he and the other sages in Dasharatha''s   court know the capabilities of Rama and though Rama is in his adolescence, he   can easily kill the demons. But …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "section_metadata", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Introduction", "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda, Sarga 19"}, {"chunk_id": "b1bc66f5-1954-483e-a7db-b00c168c90df", "confidence": 0.913333333333333, "excerpt": "Now Rama''s reputation is going to be enhanced by Sage Vishvamitra in eliminating   demoness Tataka, in releasing Ahalya from her curse, breaking Shiva''s bow, marriage   with Seetha, rebuffing Parashuraama etc. And also Vishvamitra leaves the decision   to Dasharatha, ''if only your ministers, clergy as well political, permit then   alone you send Rama with …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "curated_source_token_overlap", "page_or_section": "Introduction", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Introduction", "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda, Sarga 19"}], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "Valmiki", "authority_metadata": {}, "citation": "Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Sarga 19 (https://valmikiramayan.net/utf8/baala/sarga19/balasans19.htm)", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": null, "publisher_or_institution": "Valmiki Ramayana project", "rights_or_access_note": null, "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "scholarly_article", "storage_path": null, "title": "Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Sarga 19", "url": "https://valmikiramayan.net/utf8/baala/sarga19/balasans19.htm"}, "source_id": "826d5c8a-6135-4304-8825-09579ff9a478"}], "unmatched_passage_links": []}'::jsonb,'approved','system_test_bulk');
insert into original_claims values ('121fd80b-1ab0-4f8c-b965-8c48554342d1','{"approval_origin": "system_test_bulk", "certainty": "high", "claim_id": "121fd80b-1ab0-4f8c-b965-8c48554342d1", "claim_kind": "textual_tradition", "claim_relations": [], "claim_text": "Vishvamitra gives Dasharatha strategic information about Maricha, Subahu, and their connection to Ravana while pressing the request for Rama, so the court dialogue includes threat assessment as well as spiritual authority.", "current_status": "approved", "epistemics": {"contract_basis": null, "contract_satisfied": false, "contract_status": "NEEDS_TYPE_REVIEW", "epistemic_type": null, "proposed_type": "TRADITIONAL_NARRATIVE", "reviewer_kind": null, "type_origin": "deterministic", "type_review_status": "needs_type_review"}, "evidence_flags": ["Missing assigned epistemic classification", "Current approval origin does not establish strict verification", "Recorded source access issue"], "evidence_type": "traditional_narrative", "lens": {"field_id": "de20bc35-2696-4a80-b095-614bb650a581", "field_label": "Advisory relationships with kings or dynasties", "parameter_id": "700ce11a-bd45-4116-99a4-a246dc97c505", "parameter_name": "Relational / Social Role"}, "parent_claim_id": null, "personality_id": "2853f4ed-98b1-4ef8-9f30-30f85d42edad", "personality_name": "Vishvamitra", "reviewer_decision": null, "sources": [{"evidence_excerpt": "Sarga 20 records Vishvamitra explaining who disrupts his sacrifices and their connection with Ravana.", "link_id": "203d1ca6-748a-4c80-9411-6926b5a9d62b", "page_or_section": "1.20", "passages": [{"chunk_id": "3ffa1903-bca8-4d56-996f-54a1495215e4", "confidence": 0.873333333333333, "excerpt": "\"That formidable Ravana is not a devastator of rituals by himself, even so, two very mighty demons  called Mareecha and Subaahu will cause devastating hindrances to rituals, instigated by him.\" Thus  Sage Vishvamitra said to Dasharatha.  Thus when he is said so by that sage Vishvamitra, then the king Dasharatha spoke to the sage, \"I  myself am not capable …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "curated_source_token_overlap", "page_or_section": "Book I : Bala Kanda - The Youthful Majesties\nSarga 20 in Prose", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Book I : Bala Kanda - The Youthful Majesties\nSarga 20 in Prose", "source_id": "3295f888-388c-4d95-b768-ad4f44928a76", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda"}, {"chunk_id": "fcc0eead-cb31-4021-8270-4dae2a0ac4b1", "confidence": 0.873333333333333, "excerpt": "\"Here is the full-fledged battalion, called akshouhiNi sena , for which  I am the leader and controller, and fortified by this army I will go there to encounter those  demons.  One pankti row, is the first unit of ancient military, which consists of 1 chariot, 1  elephant, 3 cavalry, and 7-foot soldiers. Multiples of this first unit become an akshouhiNi  …", "excerpt_truncated": true, "is_active": true, "link_method": "curated_source_token_overlap", "page_or_section": "Book I : Bala Kanda - The Youthful Majesties\nSarga 20 in Prose", "relationship": "supports", "section_title": "Book I : Bala Kanda - The Youthful Majesties\nSarga 20 in Prose", "source_id": "3295f888-388c-4d95-b768-ad4f44928a76", "textual_layer": "Ramayana — Bala Kanda"}], "relationship": "supports", "source": {"author": "Valmiki (traditional attribution); English rendering by Desiraju Hanumanta Rao", "authority_metadata": {"verified_http_status": 200}, "citation": "Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda 1.20, chapter-level prose rendering.", "original_work_identity": {}, "publication_year": null, "publisher_or_institution": "Valmiki Ramayana project", "rights_or_access_note": "Publicly accessible chapter page; preserve source attribution and link.", "source_status": "approved", "source_type": "primary_text", "storage_path": null, "title": "Valmiki Ramayana — Bala Kanda, Sarga 20 (prose)", "url": "https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/baala/sarga20/bala_20_prose.htm"}, "sourc